@meta
id: 20-03
title: A register with a name
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 53
minutes: 5
vscode: a clipboard manager, if you have one installed
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
Vim has twenty-six registers named `a` to `z`, and any yank, delete or
put can be pointed at one of them by typing `"` and the letter first.
`"ayy` yanks the line into `a`. `"ap` puts it back. Nothing else touches
`a` until you tell it to, so a named register is where you keep
something you will need later, while the unnamed register churns
through everything else you do in between.

`capture` needs the same status guard `refund` has: the three-line
`if` block, with a blank line before it, after its own `const response`
line. Its code is `CAPTURE_FAILED`. The `legacy` call in `capture` is
dead and has to go.

Pick the guard up into a named register first, then do the delete, then
put. The unnamed register can do what it likes.

@keys
| `"ayy` | yank the line into register `a` |
| `"a4yy` | four lines into `a` |
| `"ap` `"aP` | put from `a`, after or before |
| `"add` | delete the line into `a` |
| `:reg a` | show what is in `a` |

@hints
The register letter goes between the `"` and the command, and the count
goes after it: `"a3yy`.
--
`/if (` Enter finds the guard. `k` steps up to the blank line above it
and `"a4yy` takes the blank and the guard together. `/legacy` Enter,
`dd`, and you are on the blank line under `const response`. `"aP` puts
the four lines above it.
--
The pasted block still says `REFUND_FAILED`. `/REF` Enter from where
the put left you lands on it, and `ct_` changes up to the underscore.

@solution
    /if (<Enter> k "a4yy  /legacy<Enter> dd  "aP  /REF<Enter> ct_CAPTURE<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`"0` would have worked here too, because the guard was the last yank.
The difference is intent. `"0` is a safety net: it happens to hold the
last yank whatever else you did. A named register is a shelf you put
something on deliberately, and it holds it until you replace it, through
any number of yanks and deletes and across every file you open in this
session.

Two things people get wrong at first. The `"` is the double-quote key,
typed once, not twice. And it goes *before* the command, including
before any count: `"a3yy`, not `3"ayy` (which also works, as it happens,
but stop reading vim's leniency as a rule).

@start payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/captures`, { orderId, amount });
    const legacy = await this.http.post(`/v1/captures`, { orderId, amount });

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async capture(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/captures`, { orderId, amount });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('CAPTURE_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
